{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>And the live bird, etc.</b> So that it goes dyed and walks over the face of the field, and its fellows of the same species recognize it, see that it has changed, gather around it, and kill it — so that no other leper finds it and offers it, for the Merciful One said וכפר עליו — 'and it shall make atonement for him' — and not for another. And so our Rabbis said: the bird returns, the tzara'at returns. A proof for the matter: 'the dyed bird has become my inheritance' — and where does the Torah speak of a dyed bird, if not in this matter? However, the Talmud proves that it is permitted by reasoning: could the Torah have said 'send' for a stumbling-block?"], :book :leviticus, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {0 "The phrase העיט צבוע נחלתי לי ('the dyed bird has become my inheritance') appears to be a citation or allusion whose exact source is uncertain; rendered hyper-literally as it stands."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:12:09.652178135Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}